# You land in less without asking for it: git log, git diff, man, and anything
# else long. "How do I get out of this" is the first question it raises.
# Its keys are compiled in, and `less --help` lists only command-line flags —
# so there is nothing to probe, and every row here is literal.
name:    less
what:    the pager you land in from git log and man. q gets you out
check:   less
docs:    h inside it

= GETTING OUT
q                | quit
Ctrl-c           | if q is not working, something else has the keyboard

= MOVING
Down / Up        | one line
Space or PgDn    | one screen forward
b or PgUp        | one screen back
g                | the very top
G                | the very bottom, which also loads the whole file
Enter            | one line forward
d / u            | half a screen down / up
mouse scroll     | works in most terminals

= SEARCHING
/text            | search forwards
?text            | search backwards
n                | next match
N                | previous match
&text            | show ONLY the lines that match. Very useful in a long log
Esc u            | clear the search highlight

= WHILE WATCHING A FILE GROW
F                | follow, like tail -f. Ctrl-c to stop following
R                | re-read the file from disk

= LAYOUT
-S then Enter    | stop wrapping long lines; use Right/Left to scroll instead
-N then Enter    | show line numbers
h                | the built-in help, which lists everything
